{"parentUuid":"25e5056a-4ce1-42a1-8e89-2fab17f909db","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/jhonortiz/devops/tf-infrastructure","sessionId":"2ae41cd2-55ba-4d99-8253-562627d8ca39","version":"2.1.45","gitBranch":"master","agentId":"a525a7f","slug":"sequential-wiggling-tome","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"REDACTED","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now I have a thorough understanding of your current setup. Here is the comprehensive research report based on my knowledge (with the caveat that web search/fetch tools were unavailable, so I cannot verify the very latest releases from after my training cutoff).\n\n---\n\n# Terraform Upgrade Research Report\n\n## Your Current State\n\nBased on examining your repository at `/home/jhonortiz/devops/tf-infrastructure`:\n\n- **Terraform version constraint**: `~> 1.11` (across all 33+ root modules)\n- **Google provider**: `hashicorp/google` version `~> 6.26` (locked at `6.27.0`)\n- **Cloudflare provider**: `cloudflare/cloudflare` version `~> 4.0` (locked at `4.52.0`)\n- **SOPS provider**: `carlpett/sops` version `~> 1.1` (locked at `1.2.0`)\n- **Architecture**: 33+ independent root modules (not a single monolith), each with their own `versions.tf`, `providers.tf`, `backend.tf`, and `.terraform.lock.hcl`\n\n---\n\n## 1. Terraform Version Changes: 1.11.x to 1.14.5\n\n**Important caveat**: As of my training data (May 2025), Terraform had released up to version **1.12.x** (in preview/early release). Versions 1.13 and 1.14 may not yet exist or may be very recent. You should verify the actual latest version at:\n- https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/releases\n- https://releases.hashicorp.com/terraform/\n\n### Terraform 1.12 (Released ~Q2 2025)\n\nKey changes from 1.11:\n\n**New Features:**\n- **`ephemeral` values**: A new concept for sensitive values that are never persisted to state. Ephemeral variables (`ephemeral = true`), ephemeral outputs, and ephemeral resources were introduced. This builds on work started in 1.10/1.11.\n- **`for_each` for providers**: You can now use `for_each` on provider configurations, a long-requested feature. This enables dynamic provider instantiation.\n- **Improved `moved` blocks**: Enhanced support for refactoring, including moving resources across modules.\n- **State encryption enhancements** (for backends that support it in OpenTofu/HCP Terraform).\n\n**Breaking Changes / Deprecations:**\n- The legacy `terraform plan -detailed-exitcode` behavior was refined; scripts relying on specific exit codes should be tested.\n- Some previously deprecated CLI flags were removed.\n- `terraform fmt` may format some configurations slightly differently (cosmetic).\n- Provider protocol v5 minimum: older providers that only support protocol v4 may not work.\n\n### Terraform 1.13 and 1.14\n\n**These versions were not yet released as of my training cutoff (May 2025).** If they now exist, you must check the changelogs directly:\n\n- https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\n- https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/releases\n\n**If Terraform 1.14.5 does not actually exist yet**, the version you may be thinking of could be:\n- An OpenTofu version (OpenTofu has different versioning)\n- A Terraform Cloud/Enterprise version\n- A future release\n\n**Action required**: Please run `terraform version` to confirm your current installed version, and verify that `1.14.5` exists at https://releases.hashicorp.com/terraform/\n\n### Changelogs to Check\n\n| Version | Changelog URL |\n|---------|--------------|\n| 1.11.x | https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/releases/tag/v1.11.2 |\n| 1.12.x | https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/releases/tag/v1.12.0 |\n| 1.13.x | https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/releases (search for v1.13) |\n| 1.14.x | https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/releases (search for v1.14) |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Latest Google Provider Version\n\n### As of May 2025\n\n- **Latest stable `hashicorp/google`**: **6.x** series (your lockfile shows `6.27.0`)\n- **Latest stable `hashicorp/google-beta`**: Tracks the same version as the GA provider\n\nYour current constraint `~> 6.26` allows any `6.x >= 6.26.0`, so you are already on a recent version.\n\n### Breaking Changes from 5.x to 6.x\n\nThe Google provider 6.0 was a major release with significant breaking changes:\n\n1. **Removed deprecated resources and data sources**:\n   - `google_compute_shared_vpc_host_project` replaced by `google_compute_shared_vpc_host_project` (renamed)\n   - Multiple resources had fields removed that were deprecated in 5.x\n\n2. **Default behavior changes**:\n   - `deletion_protection` now defaults to `true` for many resources (Cloud SQL, GKE clusters, etc.)\n   - `google_container_cluster` removed support for legacy ABAC\n   - Default network tier changes in some resources\n\n3. **Field renames and restructuring**:\n   - Several resources had nested blocks converted to attributes or vice versa\n   - `google_project_service` behavior changes around `disable_on_destroy`\n\n4. **Removed deprecated fields**:\n   - `google_container_cluster`: removed `enable_binary_authorization` (use `binary_authorization` block)\n   - `google_compute_instance`: removed several deprecated network interface fields\n   - Many resources had `self_link` output changes\n\n5. **Provider configuration changes**:\n   - `user_project_override` behavior was clarified\n   - Some default timeout values changed\n\n**Since you're already on 6.26+, you've already absorbed the 5.x to 6.x breaking changes.** To find the absolute latest version, check:\n- https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/google/latest\n- https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-google/releases\n\n---\n\n## 3. Latest Cloudflare Provider Version\n\n### Your current state\nYour lockfile shows **Cloudflare provider 4.52.0** with constraint `~> 4.0`.\n\n### Cloudflare Provider 5.x (Major Release)\n\n**As of my training data, the Cloudflare provider 5.0 was released or was imminent.** This is a **complete rewrite** of the provider:\n\n**Critical Breaking Changes in 5.x:**\n\n1. **Complete API rewrite**: The provider was rewritten from scratch using a new code generation framework (terraform-plugin-framework instead of terraform-plugin-sdk).\n\n2. **Resource renames**: Many resources were renamed to follow a more consistent naming convention:\n   - `cloudflare_record` may become `cloudflare_dns_record`\n   - `cloudflare_access_application` may change structure\n   - Multiple WAF-related resources were restructured\n\n3. **Attribute changes**: Many attributes were renamed or restructured across resources.\n\n4. **Removed resources**: Several deprecated resources were removed entirely.\n\n5. **State migration**: Due to the rewrite, state migration may require manual intervention for some resources.\n\n**This is a very high-impact upgrade.** The Cloudflare team published an upgrade guide:\n- https://github.com/cloudflare/terraform-provider-cloudflare/blob/main/MIGRATION.md\n- https://registry.terraform.io/providers/cloudflare/cloudflare/latest/docs/guides/version-5-upgrade\n\n**Recommendation**: Do NOT upgrade from 4.x to 5.x without thorough testing. The `~> 4.0` constraint in your code correctly prevents an accidental upgrade to 5.x.\n\nCheck the latest version:\n- https://registry.terraform.io/providers/cloudflare/cloudflare/latest\n- https://github.com/cloudflare/terraform-provider-cloudflare/releases\n\n---\n\n## 4. Best Practices for Terraform Provider Version Management\n\n### Where to Pin Versions\n\n**Recommended approach: Pin in root modules, use flexible constraints in shared/child modules.**\n\nYour current setup already follows a pattern where each root module (e.g., `networking/dns/`, `apps/gke/`, etc.) has its own `versions.tf`. This is actually a good foundation, but there are improvements to consider:\n\n#### Current Pattern in Your Repo (Repeated per Root Module)\n```hcl\nterraform {\n  required_version = \"~> 1.11\"\n  required_providers {\n    google = {\n      source  = \"hashicorp/google\"\n      version = \"~>6.26\"\n    }\n    cloudflare = {\n      source  = \"cloudflare/cloudflare\"\n      version = \"~> 4.0\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### Best Practices\n\n1. **Root modules should use tight constraints**: Use the pessimistic constraint operator (`~>`) with specific patch versions for production stability:\n   ```hcl\n   # In root modules\n   google = {\n     source  = \"hashicorp/google\"\n     version = \"~> 6.27.0\"  # Allows 6.27.x but not 6.28.0\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. **Child/shared modules should use wide constraints**: Reusable modules should specify the minimum version they require but not be overly restrictive:\n   ```hcl\n   # In reusable modules\n   google = {\n     source  = \"hashicorp/google\"\n     version = \">= 6.0\"\n   }\n   ```\n\n3. **Always commit `.terraform.lock.hcl`**: You are already doing this. The lockfile provides reproducible builds regardless of the constraint syntax.\n\n4. **Use `required_version` consistently**: Your `~> 1.11` constraint is appropriate -- it allows 1.11.x but not 1.12+. When upgrading, update this across all modules.\n\n5. **Centralized version management options**:\n   - **Option A (your current approach)**: Each root module manages its own versions. Simple but leads to version drift between modules.\n   - **Option B**: Use a shared `.tf` file via symlinks or a code generation tool to maintain versions in one place.\n   - **Option C**: Use Terragrunt with a centralized `generate` block to inject provider versions into all modules.\n   - **Option D**: Use a CI/CD pipeline that validates all modules use the same provider versions.\n\n6. **For your specific repo**: Since you have 33+ root modules all with nearly identical `versions.tf` files, consider a script or CI check that ensures all modules specify the same provider versions.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Terraform Upgrade Path from 1.11.2 to 1.14.5\n\n### General Terraform Upgrade Rules\n\nHashiCorp's official guidance:\n\n1. **You can skip minor versions**: Terraform guarantees state file forward compatibility. You can upgrade from 1.11 directly to 1.14 without stopping at 1.12 and 1.13.\n\n2. **However, incremental upgrades are RECOMMENDED** because:\n   - Each minor version may introduce deprecation warnings for features that will be removed in the next version\n   - Running `terraform plan` at each version lets you catch issues incrementally\n   - State file format upgrades happen transparently, but once upgraded, you cannot downgrade\n\n3. **You CANNOT downgrade**: Once you run `terraform plan` or `terraform apply` with a newer version, the state file is upgraded and older versions may refuse to read it.\n\n### Recommended Upgrade Procedure\n\n**Step 1: Preparation**\n```\n# Back up ALL state files before starting\n# For each root module, either back up the GCS state bucket or use terraform state pull\n```\n\n**Step 2: Upgrade incrementally (recommended)**\n```\n1.11.2 -> 1.12.x (latest patch) -> 1.13.x (latest patch) -> 1.14.5\n```\n\nAt each version:\n1. Install the new Terraform binary\n2. Update `required_version` in all `versions.tf` files\n3. Run `terraform init -upgrade` in each root module\n4. Run `terraform plan` and check for warnings/errors\n5. Address any deprecation warnings before moving to the next version\n6. Run `terraform apply` only after confirming the plan is clean\n\n**Step 3: Update provider constraints if needed**\n```\n# After Terraform is upgraded, separately upgrade providers\nterraform init -upgrade\nterraform plan\n```\n\n**Step 4: For your specific repo, update all 33+ modules**\n\nGiven that you have many root modules, the upgrade should be done module-by-module or with a script:\n\n```bash\n# For each module directory:\nfor dir in org/projects iam/permissions networking/dns ...; do\n  cd \"$dir\"\n  # Update required_version in versions.tf\n  terraform init -upgrade\n  terraform plan\n  cd -\ndone\n```\n\n### Critical Warnings\n\n1. **State file backup is non-negotiable**: Your state lives in GCS (`liftit-tf-state`). Take a snapshot of the entire bucket before starting.\n\n2. **CI/CD pipeline**: If you have CI/CD running Terraform, all runners must be updated to the new version simultaneously, or you risk state file version conflicts.\n\n3. **Lock file regeneration**: After upgrading Terraform, run `terraform init -upgrade` to regenerate `.terraform.lock.hcl` files. These should be committed.\n\n4. **Workspaces**: Your repo uses `terraform.workspace` in provider configs. Ensure you test the upgrade in all workspaces (not just the default).\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Component | Current Version | Target/Latest | Risk Level | Key Action |\n|-----------|----------------|---------------|------------|------------|\n| Terraform | 1.11.x | 1.14.5 (verify exists) | Medium | Incremental upgrade, back up state |\n| hashicorp/google | 6.27.0 | Check latest 6.x | Low | Already on 6.x, minor update |\n| cloudflare/cloudflare | 4.52.0 | 5.x available | **HIGH** | Complete provider rewrite; do NOT upgrade without migration plan |\n| carlpett/sops | 1.2.0 | Check latest | Low | Stable provider, minimal changes |\n\n## URLs to Verify (since I could not fetch live data)\n\n1. **Terraform releases**: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/releases\n2. **Terraform changelog**: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md\n3. **Google provider releases**: https://github.com/hashicorp/terraform-provider-google/releases\n4. **Google provider 6.0 upgrade guide**: https://registry.terraform.io/providers/hashicorp/google/latest/docs/guides/version_6_upgrade\n5. **Cloudflare provider releases**: https://github.com/cloudflare/terraform-provider-cloudflare/releases\n6. **Cloudflare 5.x migration guide**: https://registry.terraform.io/providers/cloudflare/cloudflare/latest/docs/guides/version-5-upgrade\n7. **Terraform upgrade guides**: https://deve